---
components: ["SmallMultiples"]
description: Small multiples lay out one chart per facet on shared scales, so the eye compares shapes across a grid instead of lines tangled in one plot.
---

<script>
  import { AreaChart, BarChart, LineChart, SmallMultiples } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const DAY = 86_400_000;
  const START = new Date(2026, 0, 1).getTime();
  const REGIONS = ["EMEA", "APAC", "AMER", "LATAM", "MEA", "ANZ"];
  const revenue = REGIONS.flatMap((region, r) =>
    Array.from({ length: 24 }, (_, i) => ({
      date: new Date(START + i * 14 * DAY),
      region,
      revenue: Math.round(20000 + r * 6000 + Math.sin(i / 4 + r) * 8000 + ((i * 7919 * (r + 1)) % 3000)),
    })),
  );
  const quarters = ["Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"].flatMap((quarter, q) =>
    REGIONS.slice(0, 3).map((region, r) => ({ quarter, region, deals: 12 + q * 4 + r * 7 })),
  );
</script>

`SmallMultiples` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

It splits `data` by `facet` and renders the default slot once per facet, in a grid. The slot receives the facet's `rows` and, when `x` and `y` are given, a `yDomain` and an `xDomain` shared by every facet, plus a `syncId` so hover follows across the charts. Pass those to whichever chart you put inside.

## Basic

Set `facet`, `x`, and `y`. Every chart then shares one y scale from zero to the largest value anywhere, so a tall line means the same thing in every cell, and hovering one chart shows the same x in all of them.

<SmallMultiples data={revenue} facet="region" x="date" y="revenue" columns={3} title="Revenue by region" let:facet let:rows let:yDomain let:xDomain let:syncId>
  <LineChart data={rows} x="date" y="revenue" title={facet} {yDomain} {xDomain} {syncId} height={160} legend={false} />
</SmallMultiples>

## Columns

Set `columns` for the grid. It collapses to one column on a narrow screen.

<SmallMultiples data={revenue} facet="region" x="date" y="revenue" columns={2} title="Revenue by region, two across" let:facet let:rows let:yDomain let:xDomain let:syncId>
  <AreaChart data={rows} x="date" y="revenue" title={facet} {yDomain} {xDomain} {syncId} height={140} legend={false} />
</SmallMultiples>

## Independent scales

Set `sharedY` to `false` to let each chart fit its own data, when the shapes matter more than the sizes. Set `zero` to `false` to let the shared domain start above zero.

<SmallMultiples data={revenue} facet="region" x="date" y="revenue" sharedY={false} title="Revenue by region, each to its own scale" let:facet let:rows let:xDomain let:syncId>
  <LineChart data={rows} x="date" y="revenue" title={facet} {xDomain} {syncId} height={160} legend={false} zero={false} />
</SmallMultiples>

## Any chart

The slot can hold any chart. A categorical x has no shared domain, so pass only `yDomain`; hover still follows across the charts by category when they share `syncId`.

<SmallMultiples data={quarters} facet="region" y="deals" columns={3} title="Deals by quarter and region" let:facet let:rows let:yDomain let:syncId>
  <BarChart data={rows} x="quarter" y="deals" title={facet} {yDomain} {syncId} height={160} legend={false} />
</SmallMultiples>

## Accessibility

Each chart keeps its own name, its own tab stop, and its own data table, as it would on its own. The grid's `title` is a caption over the set. Hover shared across the charts is a pointer convenience; the keyboard reads each chart on its own.
